```sql
--Description / Thinking process:
-- Goal: Inspect movie release years without casting errors.
-- Problem: The "year" column sometimes contains text like 'I 2009', which breaks a simple ::INTEGER cast.
-- Solution steps:
--   a. REGEXP_SUBSTR("year", '[0-9]{4}') extracts the first 4-digit sequence (assumed to be the year).
--   b. TRY_TO_NUMBER(...) converts that substring to a number, returning NULL instead of raising an error if conversion fails.
--   c. ORDER BY this numeric value so rows are chronologically sorted; NULLS LAST parks any unparseable rows at the end.
--   d. Use DISTINCT to avoid duplicate movie rows and LIMIT 20 for a concise sample.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "MID",
       "title",
       "year"
FROM DB_IMDB.DB_IMDB."MOVIE"
ORDER BY TRY_TO_NUMBER(REGEXP_SUBSTR("year", '[0-9]{4}')) NULLS LAST,
         "year"
LIMIT 20;
```
